WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.467 --> 00:00:03.902
-Hi, Stacey. How are you?
-I am well. How are you, Seth?

00:00:03.902 --> 00:00:05.104
-I'm doing very well.

00:00:05.104 --> 00:00:06.906
We last spoke here
in the studio in February,

00:00:06.906 --> 00:00:08.775
the night of
the New Hampshire primary.

00:00:08.775 --> 00:00:11.978
Certainly, a lot
has changed since then.

00:00:11.978 --> 00:00:13.212
-We still have
a country, though.

00:00:13.212 --> 00:00:14.680
-Yeah. You know what?

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:17.884
It was a coin flip for a while,
but we still have a country.

00:00:17.884 --> 00:00:20.753
And, certainly, one of the most
important things

00:00:20.753 --> 00:00:21.921
to a country like this

00:00:21.921 --> 00:00:25.558
is everyone's
free and fair right to vote.

00:00:25.558 --> 00:00:28.194
You made a documentary about
voter suppression

00:00:28.194 --> 00:00:30.330
over the years up to
and including

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:33.932
your gubernatorial election
in Georgia in 2018.

00:00:33.932 --> 00:00:36.568
Was this something that
you ever saw yourself doing,

00:00:36.568 --> 00:00:38.438
making a documentary?

00:00:38.438 --> 00:00:39.739
-No, not at all.

00:00:39.739 --> 00:00:43.042
I had actually been approached
by a few filmmakers

00:00:43.042 --> 00:00:44.577
who were interested in doing
a documentary

00:00:44.577 --> 00:00:46.179
about the 2018 election.

00:00:46.179 --> 00:00:47.313
I was there.

00:00:47.313 --> 00:00:49.181
I had no interest
in reliving it.

00:00:49.181 --> 00:00:53.086
But it gave me the idea of
doing a documentary

00:00:53.086 --> 00:00:54.988
that didn't focus on me
as a politician,

00:00:54.988 --> 00:00:57.023
but really focused on the voters

00:00:57.023 --> 00:01:00.759
and understanding the history
of voter suppression

00:01:00.759 --> 00:01:03.195
so we could understand
its current impact

00:01:03.195 --> 00:01:05.431
and we could mitigate
its future effect.

00:01:05.431 --> 00:01:07.633
And, so, I actually
went shopping.

00:01:07.633 --> 00:01:11.838
I found an amazing
producer/filmmaking partner

00:01:11.838 --> 00:01:13.339
in Liz Garbus and Dan Cogan.

00:01:13.339 --> 00:01:15.207
They brought on board
Lisa Cortés.

00:01:15.207 --> 00:01:16.409
I got out of the way,

00:01:16.409 --> 00:01:18.544
and they made this
extraordinary documentary.

00:01:18.544 --> 00:01:20.946
And they tricked me into
being in it anyway.

00:01:20.946 --> 00:01:23.082
But I think
they did a great job.

00:01:23.082 --> 00:01:25.418
-One of the things the
documentary tries to lay out

00:01:25.418 --> 00:01:28.721
is that what happened in 2018,
what's happening now --

00:01:28.721 --> 00:01:29.822
this is not new.

00:01:29.822 --> 00:01:31.758
A lot of it was
born out of Jim Crow.

00:01:31.758 --> 00:01:34.928
Did you find that this
was something people

00:01:34.928 --> 00:01:36.929
were educated about
or do you think

00:01:36.929 --> 00:01:39.064
it was new to a lot of people?

00:01:39.064 --> 00:01:42.568
-I think it's not only new,
but it's necessary reconnection.

00:01:42.568 --> 00:01:46.472
In the sort of DNA of America,
we had to understand that

00:01:46.472 --> 00:01:50.843
voter suppression was baked into
how our country was started.

00:01:50.843 --> 00:01:52.411
But what really
cemented it for me

00:01:52.411 --> 00:01:55.748
was a conversation with
one of my young staffers.

00:01:55.748 --> 00:01:58.484
We were talking,
after the 2018 election,

00:01:58.484 --> 00:02:00.219
about what had happened
and we were talking about

00:02:00.219 --> 00:02:02.655
what was going on in Florida
with Desmond Meade

00:02:02.655 --> 00:02:05.692
and his attempt to restore
voting rights

00:02:05.692 --> 00:02:08.228
for ex-offenders
and returning citizens.

00:02:08.228 --> 00:02:09.295
And she said, "Well, why do they

00:02:09.295 --> 00:02:11.197
keep using the phrase
'poll tax'?

00:02:11.197 --> 00:02:13.699
Isn't there a sexier way
to describe it?"

00:02:13.699 --> 00:02:15.034
And it hit me
like a ton of bricks --

00:02:15.034 --> 00:02:17.703
she had no idea that poll taxes

00:02:17.703 --> 00:02:22.107
were legitimately taxes
that were imposed on others.

00:02:22.107 --> 00:02:24.076
And the more we talked,
the more I remembered

00:02:24.076 --> 00:02:26.579
I'm the daughter of
civil-rights activists.

00:02:26.579 --> 00:02:30.583
If you're a Millennial,
if you are a Gen Z,

00:02:30.583 --> 00:02:31.851
you've only ever lived with

00:02:31.851 --> 00:02:34.153
the Voting Rights Act
being in place.

00:02:34.153 --> 00:02:36.922
And you may have seen
"Eyes on the Prize" in class,

00:02:36.922 --> 00:02:40.059
but you haven't really been
steeped in the story of

00:02:40.059 --> 00:02:43.395
how hard it has been to,
first, expand the right to vote,

00:02:43.395 --> 00:02:45.031
and now to defend it.

00:02:45.031 --> 00:02:48.601
-You mentioned, obviously,
your parents helped connect you

00:02:48.601 --> 00:02:51.404
to the history, but it seems
that you have always been

00:02:51.404 --> 00:02:53.606
pretty connected to the history
up to and including --

00:02:53.606 --> 00:02:55.608
You spoke at the
30th anniversary of

00:02:55.608 --> 00:02:57.543
the March on Washington.

00:02:57.543 --> 00:03:00.280
That is --
You're only 19 years old.

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:03.616
I imagine, at that age --
at any age --

00:03:03.616 --> 00:03:06.785
there's a lot of pressure at a
speaking engagement like that.

00:03:06.785 --> 00:03:09.255
How did it feel?
-I don't remember it.

00:03:09.255 --> 00:03:11.690
I'm fairly certain
I blanked out.

00:03:11.690 --> 00:03:13.359
That's a lot of people.
-Yeah.

00:03:13.359 --> 00:03:17.062
-And, so, I remember getting out
of the car, going, you know,

00:03:17.062 --> 00:03:20.767
through the process
getting up there,

00:03:20.767 --> 00:03:23.168
and I looked out over
the Reflecting Pool

00:03:23.168 --> 00:03:25.404
and saw all of those
thousands of people.

00:03:25.404 --> 00:03:27.106
And then I remember being done.

00:03:27.106 --> 00:03:29.341
My mom actually --
We talked that night.

00:03:29.341 --> 00:03:30.810
This was before cellphones,
so I had to wait till

00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:33.646
I got back to the hotel
and call her from a landline.

00:03:33.646 --> 00:03:35.548
And she said, "How did it go?"
I'm like, "I don't know.

00:03:35.548 --> 00:03:36.882
I don't remember.

00:03:36.882 --> 00:03:40.119
I just remember there being
a lot of people there."

00:03:40.119 --> 00:03:41.554
-Well, you know, trial by fire.

00:03:41.554 --> 00:03:43.222
It's very important, obviously,
early on

00:03:43.222 --> 00:03:44.791
to learn what it's like
being in front of

00:03:44.791 --> 00:03:46.358
a group of people.

00:03:46.358 --> 00:03:48.361
You know, obviously, a lot of
people are talking about

00:03:48.361 --> 00:03:51.630
how this
election will be conducted,

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:53.232
the level of fairness involved.

00:03:53.232 --> 00:03:55.635
It seems like the president
is doing a lot of work

00:03:55.635 --> 00:03:58.905
to sow doubt about things
like mail-in voting,

00:03:58.905 --> 00:04:03.142
obviously making it harder for
the Post Office to do their job.

00:04:03.142 --> 00:04:06.779
Do you feel as though
people should be panicked

00:04:06.779 --> 00:04:09.949
or do you think there's a way to
approach this in the coming,

00:04:09.949 --> 00:04:11.250
you know, month and a half
to make sure

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:14.087
that we do have a fair election?

00:04:14.087 --> 00:04:16.989
-We can do nothing about
the imposter in chief

00:04:16.989 --> 00:04:18.157
that sits in The White House.

00:04:18.157 --> 00:04:20.392
We can't control
what he says or what he does,

00:04:20.392 --> 00:04:23.997
but we can control
our response and our power.

00:04:23.997 --> 00:04:26.332
And, so, first, don't panic.

00:04:26.332 --> 00:04:28.067
We know they're going to
keep doing things.

00:04:28.067 --> 00:04:32.437
We know that he is going to try
his best to bully and cheat us

00:04:32.437 --> 00:04:35.741
out of an election, but it can't
work if we do our part.

00:04:35.741 --> 00:04:37.377
So, first, don't panic,

00:04:37.377 --> 00:04:40.246
because when we panic, we miss
things and we make mistakes.

00:04:40.246 --> 00:04:43.850
Number two, make a plan to vote
and make it early.

00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:46.018
In 80% of the states
in our country,

00:04:46.018 --> 00:04:48.387
you can vote by mail
with no excuses,

00:04:48.387 --> 00:04:50.823
and you can start applying
for it now.

00:04:50.823 --> 00:04:54.126
We have this amazing website
called allinforvoting.com.

00:04:54.126 --> 00:04:57.062
It's a project that
we did through the film

00:04:57.062 --> 00:04:59.632
and in partnership with Amazon.

00:04:59.632 --> 00:05:02.235
It is an amazing set of
resources and tools.

00:05:02.235 --> 00:05:03.703
You can do everything
from register,

00:05:03.703 --> 00:05:05.705
to get your vote-by-mail
application,

00:05:05.705 --> 00:05:09.207
to find out your early-voting
rules and your polling location.

00:05:09.207 --> 00:05:11.109
So, plan to vote by mail.

00:05:11.109 --> 00:05:13.779
If that doesn't work,
plan to vote early in person.

00:05:13.779 --> 00:05:16.149
And if that doesn't work, have a
backup to your backup plan

00:05:16.149 --> 00:05:18.484
and know where to go
and vote on Election Day.

00:05:18.484 --> 00:05:21.687
So, one, don't panic.
Two, make a plan to vote early.

00:05:21.687 --> 00:05:24.190
And, three, don't do it alone.

00:05:24.190 --> 00:05:25.925
We need to make certain
that everyone

00:05:25.925 --> 00:05:29.162
we know has a plan to vote,
that they tell you what it is.

00:05:29.162 --> 00:05:30.896
Don't let them say, "Oh, yeah,
I'm going to make my plan."

00:05:30.896 --> 00:05:33.532
Make them tell you what it is,
make them write it down,

00:05:33.532 --> 00:05:35.134
because if we can
overwhelm the system

00:05:35.134 --> 00:05:37.937
with our presence, we will win.

00:05:37.937 --> 00:05:42.408
-You know, obviously, you had a
very close election in 2018.

00:05:42.408 --> 00:05:46.011
You know, there is a chance that
maybe all of a sudden, you know,

00:05:46.011 --> 00:05:47.713
Georgia is a swing state.

00:05:47.713 --> 00:05:51.984
How are you feeling about
Vice President Biden's chances

00:05:51.984 --> 00:05:54.019
as we're, you know,
40-some days out?

00:05:54.019 --> 00:05:55.455
-I think he can win here.

00:05:55.455 --> 00:05:58.724
We've got 16 electoral votes
we're willing to send his way.

00:05:58.724 --> 00:06:00.993
We've got two Senate seats
that are up for grabs --

00:06:00.993 --> 00:06:03.629
Jon Ossoff running against
David Perdue,

00:06:03.629 --> 00:06:05.831
Raphael Warnock running against
Kelly Loeffler.

00:06:05.831 --> 00:06:08.867
And with the right investment
and the right turnout,

00:06:08.867 --> 00:06:10.803
we can pull both of those
Senate seats

00:06:10.803 --> 00:06:12.504
over to the Democratic column.

00:06:12.504 --> 00:06:14.974
We can also send
Lucy McBath back

00:06:14.974 --> 00:06:17.810
and send her friend, in
Carolyn Bourdeaux, in the 7th.

00:06:17.810 --> 00:06:19.679
And we can flip 16 House seats

00:06:19.679 --> 00:06:21.280
and take the House
for Democrats.

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:24.716
These are all within reach,
because in 2018, without trying,

00:06:24.716 --> 00:06:27.153
we flipped 16 seats
in the General Assembly.

00:06:27.153 --> 00:06:29.522
We can do it again.
We're a narrow state.

00:06:29.522 --> 00:06:30.923
It's a very close election.

00:06:30.923 --> 00:06:33.425
And with the Secretary of State
controlling everything,

00:06:33.425 --> 00:06:36.695
he barely eked out
a 55,000-vote victory.

00:06:36.695 --> 00:06:42.468
We've had 750,000 new people
register to vote since 2018.

00:06:42.468 --> 00:06:44.803
45% are under the age of 30.

00:06:44.803 --> 00:06:47.005
49% are people of color.

00:06:47.005 --> 00:06:49.842
And we've got really good
progressive white folks

00:06:49.842 --> 00:06:51.510
in the state of Georgia
joining together.

00:06:51.510 --> 00:06:53.579
And we have the ability
to win this election

00:06:53.579 --> 00:06:56.182
and deliver Georgia
as a blue state.

00:06:56.182 --> 00:06:57.883
-That's certainly
nice optimism to hear.

00:06:57.883 --> 00:06:59.585
I do want to close.
I know you're busy.

00:06:59.585 --> 00:07:01.186
I know you've got
a million things going on.

00:07:01.186 --> 00:07:03.889
But as an accomplished author
and as someone I know

00:07:03.889 --> 00:07:07.693
who loves TV and
films, what have you been doing

00:07:07.693 --> 00:07:09.962
these last six months to unwind?

00:07:09.962 --> 00:07:12.465
Anything good
we should know about?

00:07:12.465 --> 00:07:16.536
-So, I just discovered a show,
"Body of Proof."

00:07:16.536 --> 00:07:17.937
I really like crime dramas.

00:07:17.937 --> 00:07:21.473
I just finished re-watching all
of "Elementary"

00:07:21.473 --> 00:07:23.709
and discovered, because Hulu
recommended it for me,

00:07:23.709 --> 00:07:24.810
"Body of Proof."

00:07:24.810 --> 00:07:26.211
It's a pretty good show.
-Okay. Great.

00:07:26.211 --> 00:07:27.480
-Alright, "Body of Proof."

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:30.082
That's all we're looking for is
recommendations

00:07:30.082 --> 00:07:33.719
of, one, good crime shows,
and, two, how to save democracy,

00:07:33.719 --> 00:07:36.188
and you always provide us both.

00:07:36.188 --> 00:07:38.224
So good to see you, Stacey.
Always a pleasure.

